Yices relink kit
================

You do not need this kit to install or run Yices
-------------------------------------------------

If you want to use Yices, download the normal Yices distribution archive and
follow the instructions in its README. Nothing in this kit is required to
install Yices, to run the command-line tools, or to link a program against the
Yices library. You can ignore it entirely.

This kit exists for one purpose. Some of the libraries built into the Yices
binaries are distributed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, which
gives you the right to modify those libraries and rebuild the Yices programs
against your modified versions. The kit contains the material you need to do
that: the Yices object code, the dependency archives in deps/, taken from the
official build, the link commands, and the license texts and notices for the
components the kit redistributes.

The kit does not carry every input the official link used. The C library, the
startup object files and the compiler runtime archives come from the machine
you relink on. See "What this kit does not do" below.


What is in the kit
------------------

  README                     this file
  MANIFEST.sha256            checksum for every other shipped file
  BUILDINFO                  toolchain, flags, and the production link
                             commands as they were recorded
  LICENSE                    Yices' own license terms
  NOTICES                    the components in this kit and their notices
  licenses/                  license texts that accompany this kit
  executables/               Yices object code for the command-line tools
  library/                   Yices object code for the shared library
  deps/                      the dependency archives used in the official build
  verify-manifest.sh         checks the kit is intact
  relink-executables.sh      rebuilds the four command-line tools
  relink-library.sh          rebuilds the shared library


Which libraries this concerns
-----------------------------

  GNU C Library    Built into the four command-line executables. The shared
                   library does not contain it; it loads the system copy at
                   run time. The scripts take the C library from the machine
                   you relink on. See "What this kit does not do" below.

  GMP              Built into yices, yices-smt, yices-smt2, and the shared
                   library. Not present in yices-sat.

  LibPoly          Built into yices, yices-smt, yices-smt2, and the shared
                   library. Not present in yices-sat.

CUDD, CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat are also built into the Yices
binaries. This kit publishes no relinking material for them, and replacing
them is not what it is for. Their archives are here because you cannot relink
anything without them, and their notices are here because this kit
redistributes those archives. NOTICES in this kit records what each component
is and the terms its own authors distribute it under.


What you need before you start
------------------------------

A C compiler driver and a linker for the same target as the published
binaries. The target and the toolchain used for the official build are
recorded in BUILDINFO. A static C library is required for the executables,
which on most distributions is a separate package from the shared one.

The scripts do not require a particular compiler version. They take CC from
the environment and default to cc. Relinking published object files with a
different compiler from the one that produced them usually works on x86-64
Linux for C code, but it is not guaranteed, and the kit contains C++ archives
where the risk is higher. If a link fails in a way that mentions symbol
versions or standard library internals, try matching the compiler recorded in
BUILDINFO.

You do not need the Yices source tree, a build directory, or make. The scripts
work from the extracted kit alone.


What this kit does not do
-------------------------

There is no input for a C library. The scripts have no variable for a libc
archive, for startup object files, or for a sysroot, and they read no C library
from deps/. The link uses the C library, the startup files, and the toolchain
directories of the machine you run them on. Relinking against a C library you
built yourself is outside what these scripts do.


Step 1: verify the kit

    ./verify-manifest.sh

Do this before you change anything, so that a later failure is clearly your
change and not a bad download.


Step 2: replace a library

Build your modified version of GMP or LibPoly as a static archive, then either
drop it into deps/ under the existing name or point the script at it. For
example:

    GMP_A=/path/to/my/libgmp.a ./relink-executables.sh

Once you replace a file in deps/, the manifest check will fail, which is
correct and expected. Set SKIP_VERIFY=1 to continue.

These are the inputs relink-executables.sh accepts. It reads nothing else from
the environment.

    CC             compiler driver, default cc
    CFLAGS         compile and link flags, see Step 3
    STATIC_FLAG    default -static
    BIN_LDFLAGS    extra link flags, empty by default
    LIBYICES_A     default executables/libyices.a
    GMP_A          default deps/libgmp-static.a
    LIBPOLY_A      default deps/libpoly.a
    CUDD_A         default deps/libcudd.a
    CADICAL_A      default deps/libcadical.a
    CMS_A          default deps/libcryptominisat5.a
    KISSAT_A       default deps/libkissat_yices.a
    SKIP_VERIFY    set to 1 to bypass the manifest check

The first argument is the output directory. It defaults to ./relinked.

relink-library.sh accepts the same set with four differences. It has no
STATIC_FLAG and no BIN_LDFLAGS. It reads LIBYICES_PIC_A rather than
LIBYICES_A, defaulting to library/libyices-pic.a. Its GMP_A and LIBPOLY_A
default to the position-independent archives deps/libgmp-pic.a and
deps/libpicpoly.a. It adds SONAME, default libyices.so.2.8, and LIBNAME,
default libyices.so.2.8.0.


Step 3: relink

    ./relink-executables.sh            builds into ./relinked
    ./relink-library.sh                builds into ./relinked

Each script prints the command it runs before running it. Treat the
corresponding link-* entry in BUILDINFO as the reference command. The printed
command is not identical to that entry. These are the differences.

  CC defaults to cc, while BUILDINFO records gcc as the driver that produced
  the published binaries.

  CFLAGS defaults to
  -fvisibility=hidden -O3 -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-stack-protector.
  BUILDINFO records those same four flags with -Wall and -Wredundant-decls
  inserted after -fvisibility=hidden, the whole preceded by -O2 and by a
  -ffile-prefix-map option, which rewrote the source paths recorded in the
  object files when they were compiled. The two warning flags control
  diagnostics and do not change the generated code. BUILDINFO carries the
  -ffile-prefix-map value that was used.

  The scripts resolve the kit to an absolute path and print an absolute
  pathname for every object file and archive. BUILDINFO records the same
  files as kit-relative paths, such as executables/yices.o and
  deps/libcudd.a.

  The scripts write into the output directory, so the printed command ends
  with an output path such as -o ./relinked/yices. BUILDINFO records -o yices.

Setting CC and CFLAGS to the values BUILDINFO records closes the first two
differences. The last two remain. They follow from where you unpacked the kit
and where you sent the output, and no setting changes them, so compare the two
commands input by input rather than as strings. -ffile-prefix-map affects
compilation rather than linking, so passing it here changes nothing in the
output.


Two things that are easy to get wrong
--------------------------------------

The kit contains two GMP archives and they are not interchangeable.
deps/libgmp-static.a is an optimized build used by the command-line
executables. deps/libgmp-pic.a is a position-independent build with the
assembly routines disabled, used by the shared library. The executables also
use the normal deps/libpoly.a archive, while the shared library uses
deps/libpicpoly.a. These normal and PIC archives are not interchangeable.
GMP's x86-64 assembly code is not position-independent and cannot be linked
into a shared object. If you supply your own GMP or LibPoly for the shared
library, configure it for position-independent code.

yices-sat is linked differently from the other three tools. It contains
neither GMP nor LibPoly, so if you modified only one of those, yices-sat is
unaffected and the published binary remains correct. Its link list still names
the CUDD, CryptoMiniSat, CaDiCaL and Kissat archives, because the production
rule for that binary names them too. A static linker takes only the archive
members that something refers to, and the published yices-sat carries code
from none of those four. NOTICES section 3.1 records that placement. The C
library is built into yices-sat as it is into the other three, and the scripts
take it from the machine you relink on. relink-executables.sh rebuilds all
four whichever library you replaced.


The shared library
------------------

relink-library.sh sets the shared object name to libyices.so.2.8 and creates
the two symbolic links a normal installation expects:

    libyices.so.2.8   -> libyices.so.2.8.0
    libyices.so       -> libyices.so.2.8

Check the result with:

    readelf -dW relinked/libyices.so.2.8.0 | grep -E 'SONAME|NEEDED'

The shared library incorporates the PIC static CUDD archive supplied in this
kit. It does not require a CUDD shared library at run time.


Installing what you built
-------------------------

Copy the relinked files over the ones in an unpacked Yices distribution, or
place them ahead of the installed copies on your path or library search path.
The kit does not install anything and does not modify your system.


License texts and notices
-------------------------

The licenses/ directory holds license texts for some of the components this kit
redistributes. It is not a complete set. Where a component's terms are not in
licenses/, NOTICES reproduces them instead, as it does for CUDD. Version 3 of
the Lesser General Public License incorporates the General Public License by
reference, which is why both of those are present. The MIT and Apache
License 2.0 texts are present because components in deps/ are distributed under
them, and NOTICES records which component each applies to. The BSD text is
Yices' own license, which this kit also carries as LICENSE.

The set depends on the platform. A kit for a build that contains the GNU C
Library also carries the Lesser General Public License version 2.1; a kit for a
platform whose C library comes from the operating system does not, because no
C library is built in.

NOTICES, in this kit, records each third-party component the kit redistributes, where it
came from, and the terms its own authors distribute it under. It reproduces
the copyright notices supplied by CUDD, CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat,
whose archives are in deps/. For GMP and LibPoly it records that the source
carries copyright notices in individual files rather than one notice for the
project.
